  "content_units": [
    {
      "id": "u1",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Definition and members of the alkaline earth metals (Group 2)",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "**Alkaline earth metals** are a group of elements in the periodic table. They are found in **Group 2**, the second column from the left.\n\nThe alkaline earth metals are:\n\n| Symbol | Element |\n|---|---|\n| Be | Beryllium |\n| Mg | Magnesium |\n| Ca | Calcium |\n| Sr | Strontium |\n| Ba | Barium |\n| Ra | Radium |\n\nRadium is radioactive and rare, so it is less commonly studied in school chemistry.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "statement"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The definition, group placement, list of elements, and remark about radium's radioactivity and rarity are factually correct.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u2",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Origin of the name alkaline earth metals",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "- **Alkaline**: Their oxides and hydroxides form basic, or alkaline, solutions in water.\n  - Example: calcium oxide reacts with water to produce calcium hydroxide, which is alkaline.\n- **Earth**: Early scientists called substances that did not dissolve easily in water “earths.” Many compounds of these metals occur naturally in rocks and minerals.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The etymology and historical context for both 'alkaline' (forming basic oxides/hydroxides) and 'earth' (historical term for insoluble metal oxides/minerals) are accurately described.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u3",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Outer-shell electron configuration and formation of 2+ cations",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "All alkaline earth metals have **two electrons in their outer shell**.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Magnesium: 2,8,2  \n- Calcium: 2,8,8,2  \n\nBecause they have two outer electrons, they usually lose both when reacting. They form ions with a **2+ charge**.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Mg} \\rightarrow \\text{Mg}^{2+} + 2e^-\n\\]\n\nSo their compounds often contain ions such as:\n\n- Mg²⁺\n- Ca²⁺\n- Ba²⁺",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The valence electron counts, electron configurations for Mg and Ca, the half-equation for oxidation, and the resulting 2+ ionic charges are factually accurate.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u4",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "General physical and chemical properties of alkaline earth metals",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "Alkaline earth metals are:\n\n- Shiny, silvery metals when freshly cut\n- Good conductors of heat and electricity\n- Usually harder and denser than Group 1 alkali metals\n- Reactive, although generally less reactive than Group 1 metals\n- Able to form positive ions easily\n\nThey are not found as pure metals in nature because they react with oxygen, water, and other substances.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "statement"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The stated physical and chemical characteristics (appearance, electrical/thermal conductivity, density and hardness relative to Group 1, reactivity, and natural occurrence in compounds) are correct.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u5",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Trend in reactivity down Group 2 and its atomic explanation",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "Reactivity generally **increases down Group 2**:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Be} < \\text{Mg} < \\text{Ca} < \\text{Sr} < \\text{Ba}\n\\]\n\nThis happens because, down the group:\n\n1. The atoms become larger.\n2. The outer electrons are further from the nucleus.\n3. The attraction between the nucleus and the outer electrons becomes weaker.\n4. The atoms lose their two outer electrons more easily.\n\nTherefore, barium is more reactive than magnesium.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The trend of increasing reactivity down Group 2 and the underlying periodic trend explanation (increasing atomic radius, weaker electrostatic attraction, lower first and second ionization energies) are scientifically correct.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u6",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Reaction of alkaline earth metals with oxygen",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "### 1. Reaction with Oxygen\n\nThey react with oxygen to form metal oxides.\n\n\\[\n2\\text{Mg} + \\text{O}_2 \\rightarrow 2\\text{MgO}\n\\]\n\nMagnesium burns with a very bright white flame, producing magnesium oxide.\n\n**Safety note:** Never look directly at burning magnesium because the light can damage your eyes.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The reaction of Group 2 metals with oxygen to form oxides, the balanced equation for the combustion of magnesium, the bright white flame observation, and the laboratory eye-safety caution are accurate.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u7",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Reaction of alkaline earth metals with water",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "### 2. Reaction with Water\n\nThe reactivity with water increases down the group.\n\n- **Beryllium:** does not react easily with water.\n- **Magnesium:** reacts very slowly with cold water, but reacts faster with steam.\n- **Calcium:** reacts noticeably with cold water.\n- **Strontium and barium:** react vigorously with water.\n\nExample with calcium:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Ca} + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\rightarrow \\text{Ca(OH)}_2 + \\text{H}_2\n\\]\n\nThis produces:\n\n- Calcium hydroxide, an alkaline solution\n- Hydrogen gas",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The relative reactivities of Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, and Ba with water/steam, the balanced equation for calcium reacting with water, and the products formed are accurate.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u8",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Reaction of alkaline earth metals with acids",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "### 3. Reaction with Acids\n\nThey react with acids to form a salt and hydrogen gas.\n\nFor example:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Mg} + 2\\text{HCl} \\rightarrow \\text{MgCl}_2 + \\text{H}_2\n\\]\n\nMagnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid to make magnesium chloride and hydrogen.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The general reaction with acids forming salt and hydrogen, along with the balanced equation for magnesium reacting with hydrochloric acid, is accurate.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u9",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Properties and uses of magnesium oxide",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "### Magnesium Oxide, MgO\n- A white solid\n- Used in refractory materials that can withstand high temperatures\n- Found in some antacids",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "real_world",
        "treatment": "illustrative"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "everyday",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "excerpt_index": 0,
            "quote": "Found in some antacids"
          }
        ]
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "Magnesium oxide is a white solid, a refractory material due to its high melting point, and an active ingredient in antacids (e.g., milk of magnesia precursor).",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u10",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Properties, uses, and acid reaction of calcium carbonate",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "### Calcium Carbonate, CaCO₃\n- Found in limestone, marble, chalk, and seashells\n- Used in cement and building materials\n- Reacts with acids to release carbon dioxide\n\n\\[\n\\text{CaCO}_3 + 2\\text{HCl} \\rightarrow \\text{CaCl}_2 + \\text{CO}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}\n\\]",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "real_world",
        "treatment": "illustrative"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "everyday",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "excerpt_index": 0,
            "quote": "chalk, and seashells"
          }
        ]
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The natural occurrences of CaCO3, its uses in construction, and the balanced chemical equation for its reaction with hydrochloric acid are correct.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u11",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Properties and uses of calcium hydroxide",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "### Calcium Hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂\n- Also called slaked lime\n- Used to neutralize acidic soils\n- Used in water treatment and construction",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "real_world",
        "treatment": "illustrative"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "Calcium hydroxide is commonly called slaked lime, used in liming acidic soils, water purification, and construction mortar.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u12",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Properties and uses of magnesium sulfate",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "### Magnesium Sulfate, MgSO₄\n- Commonly known as Epsom salt\n- Used in some medical and agricultural applications",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "real_world",
        "treatment": "illustrative"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "Magnesium sulfate heptahydrate is commonly known as Epsom salt and is used in medicine and agriculture (fertilizer for magnesium-deficient soil).",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u13",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Flame test colours of alkaline earth metal ions",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "Some alkaline earth metals give characteristic colours in flame tests:\n\n| Metal ion | Flame colour |\n|---|---|\n| Ca²⁺ | Orange-red / brick-red |\n| Sr²⁺ | Crimson red |\n| Ba²⁺ | Apple green |\n| Mg²⁺ | No distinctive flame colour |\n\nThese colours help scientists identify metal ions.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "real_world",
        "treatment": "illustrative"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The listed flame test colours (brick-red for calcium, crimson for strontium, apple green for barium, and no distinctive flame colour for magnesium ions) are accurate.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u14",
      "kind": "CAVEAT",
      "label": "Anomalous behaviour of beryllium and group exceptions",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "Although the elements are in the same group, **beryllium** behaves differently from the others. It is smaller, harder, and less reactive. Its compounds can have more covalent character rather than being purely ionic.\n\nMagnesium is also less reactive than calcium, strontium, and barium. For example, magnesium reacts slowly with cold water, while calcium reacts more easily.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "subtype": "exception"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "Beryllium's anomalous behavior (high ionization energy, small ionic radius, strong covalent character in compounds like BeCl2) and magnesium's relative reactivity are accurately stated.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u15",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Role of magnesium in chlorophyll",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "- **Magnesium** is important in chlorophyll, the green pigment used by plants in photosynthesis.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "real_world",
        "treatment": "illustrative"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "Magnesium is the central metal ion in the porphyrin ring of chlorophyll molecules.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u16",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Biological roles of calcium in human physiology",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "- **Calcium** is essential for bones, teeth, muscles, and blood clotting.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "real_world",
        "treatment": "illustrative"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "everyday",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "excerpt_index": 0,
            "quote": "bones, teeth, muscles, and blood clotting"
          }
        ]
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "Calcium is essential for skeletal structure (hydroxyapatite in bones and teeth), muscle contraction, and the blood coagulation cascade.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u17",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Application of strontium in red pyrotechnics",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "- **Strontium** is used in fireworks to produce red colours.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "real_world",
        "treatment": "illustrative"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
